{"subject":"Pharmacology","sourceTitle":"All In One Online","title":"PrepLadder · 57.Previous Year Questions","deckName":"created by tele @Neet_pg_bot::prep::Qbank::Pharmacology::57.Previous_Year_Questions","questions":[{"id":"all-in-one-1760436536734","topic":"PrepLadder · 57.Previous Year Questions","number":1,"stem":"Match the following drugs with the conditions they are used to treat: Conditions Drugs 1. Delirium Tremens a. Naloxone 2. Acute Alcoholism b. Oxazepam 3. Smoking Cessation c. Acamprosate 4. Opioid Poisoning d. Varenicline","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1-a, 2-b, 3-c, 4-d"},{"label":"B","text":"1-b, 2-a, 3-d, 4-c"},{"label":"C","text":"1-c, 2-b, 3-a, 4-d"},{"label":"D","text":"1-b, 2-c, 3-d, 4-a"}],"answer":"D","answerText":"1-b, 2-c, 3-d, 4-a","solutionHtml":"yle=\"text-align: center;\"&amp;gt;<strong>Drugs</strong>\n\n\n<tr>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:bottom\">\n<p>Delirium Tremens</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:bottom\">\n<p><strong>Oxazepam</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:bottom\">\n<p>Acute Alcoholism</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:bottom\">\n<p><strong>Acamprosate</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:bottom\">\n<p>Smoking Cessation</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:bottom\">\n<p><strong>Varenicline</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:bottom\">\n<p>Opioid Poisoning</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:bottom\">\n<p><strong>Naloxone</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n\n<br/><br/><div class=\"quiz-references\">KD Tripathi Essentials of Medical Pharmacology, 8th Edition, Pages 421, 501<br/>Goodman and Gilman's Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics, 14th Edition, Page 246</div>","tags":""},{"id":"all-in-one-1760436536736","topic":"PrepLadder · 57.Previous Year Questions","number":2,"stem":"A patient is brought to the emergency department with signs suggest opioid poisoning. What is the antidote of choice?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Flumazenil"},{"label":"B","text":"Atropine"},{"label":"C","text":"Naloxone"},{"label":"D","text":"Physostigmine"}],"answer":"C","answerText":"Naloxone","solutionHtml":"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"e22dbf6c52169277719441ac94a69e\"&amp;gt;opioid <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"2e432a4f3c1692777181e8e244ca0e\">antagonist</span> that rapidly reverses opioid-induced <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"cfad80a0f716927771979aae100f95\">respiratory</span> depression and <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"cacca0f20e169277719222049c80a2\">miosis</span> — it is the <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"5e612ce1381692777181adc76f11e6\">antidote</span> of choice in suspected <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"e22dbf6c52169277719441ac94a69e\">opioid</span> overdose.\n\n<table cellspacing=\"0\" style=\"border-collapse:collapse\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"2\" style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<p style=\"text-align: center;\"><strong>Opioid Overdose/ Poisoning</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<p>Cause</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<p>Clinical, accidental, or suicidal overdose.</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<p>Symptoms</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<ul>\n<li>Unconsiousness/coma</li>\n<li>Miosis: Pin point pupil</li>\n<li>Respiratory depression</li>\n<li>Dry <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"9b0ae3750916927771986c15d4ff4d\">secretion</span> in the eye/mouth</li>\n<li>Urinary retention</li>\n<li>Constipation</li>\n<li>Flaccid muscles</li>\n<li>Convulsions (in children)</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<p>Management</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<p><strong>Acute overdose/poisoning:</strong> IV Naloxone (Antidote)</p>\n<p>                            ↓</p>\n<p><strong>Maintenance</strong> (Detoxification phase: To prevent withdrawal):</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Opioids: Buprenorphine, Methadone, Tramadol</li>\n<li>Non-Opioids- Clonidine, Lofexidine</li>\n</ul>\n<p>                            ↓</p>\n<p><strong>Abstinence</strong> (Prevent relapse):</p>\n<ul>\n<li>DOC: Oral Naltrexone</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Incorrect Options:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Flumazenil (Option A):</strong> Antagonizes benzodiazepines, not opioids.</li>\n<li><strong>Atropine (Option B): </strong>Anticholinergic agent - not the <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"5e612ce1381692777181adc76f11e6\">antidote</span> for opioids.</li>\n<li><strong>Physostigmine (Option D):</strong> For <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"9b4dbbb3321692777181e6fe172b88\">anticholinergic</span> overdose; not for <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"e22dbf6c52169277719441ac94a69e\">opioid</span> toxicity.</li>\n</ul><br/><br/>","tags":""}]}